// SOS kernel entry (designs 112, 140, 162).
//
// The per-arch kernel HAL (`sos/hal/<arch>/kernel/`) supplies the boot shim
// that sets up the stack, zeroes .bss and calls `kmain`, the board hooks and
// the memory-protection primitive, and the linker script that places the whole
// image; `sosrt` (`sos/rt/common/src/lib.saw`) supplies the runtime seams and
// the arena over those hooks; `core/lib.saw` is the arch-free kernel (console, `ktrap`, the
// object-op dispatch, the sosimg loader). See sos/spec.md §5b and §12.
//
// This file names no architecture either — which is the point of the seam: the
// same kernel entry boots Profile A and Profile B, and the only thing that
// changes is which HAL the build maps `hal` to.
//
// The kernel's whole job at boot is to hand control to the root server: load
// the one appended image, grant it its regions, drop to user mode. A kernel
// with no root image has nothing to run and says so — it does not exit quietly.

import kcore.{console, load_root_and_enter}
import hal

@export("kmain")
func kmain() {
    let uart = console()
    uart.write_str("SOS M1: kernel up on ")
    uart.write_str(hal.arch_name())
    uart.write_str("\n")

    // The interrupt controller comes up with EVERY LINE MASKED (design 178 M2
    // unit 1). A kernel that can hand a device line to a driver has to bring it
    // up at boot — an Interrupt object's `create` unmasks one line and nothing
    // else — and bringing it up masked is what makes that safe to do before
    // there is any driver: nothing is deliverable until something asks.
    //
    // NO TIMER IS ARMED HERE. The scheduler works without one (a `yield` and a
    // block both switch), and arming it would narrate over the process for the
    // first few ticks — see the handover protocol on `kcore`'s `Console`. The
    // preemption harness arms its own.
    hal.intc_init()

    load_root_and_enter()
}
